WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440 line:90%
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.490 line:84%
STEPHEN SMITH: So
what time is it?

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:09.050 line:84%
It's early on Easter
Sunday morning.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:13.540 line:84%
We remember that "Inferno"
started on Good Friday

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:18.400 line:84%
morning and then proceeded
through Friday night and Holy

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.680 line:90%
Saturday.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:27.250 line:84%
What rest there was, was the
kind of rest you get in hell.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:29.800 line:84%
Maybe as the old saying
goes, a bad night

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:33.440 line:84%
in a bad motel,
something like that.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.780 line:84%
When Dante and Virgil
emerge now at the beginning

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:41.120 line:84%
of "Purgatorio" it
is into this new day

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.627 line:84%
and to this moment near
dawn on Easter Sunday.

00:00:45.627 --> 00:00:47.210 line:84%
It's interesting to
think, by the way,

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:51.950 line:84%
of the "Inferno" as a
journey that takes place

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:54.080 line:90%
across Holy Saturday.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:57.260 line:84%
Students, of course,
love to point out

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:01.340 line:84%
that Dante's quest
resembles one of their more

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:03.890 line:84%
frequent, and
frequently lamented,

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:06.660 line:90%
practices, the all-nighter.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:11.990 line:84%
So when Dante and Virgil
emerge, it's after that journey

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:15.440 line:84%
and now finally on the
shores of Purgatorio.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:20.480 line:84%
Let's jump in to Canto 1 and
notice a few things together.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.710 line:84%
"I will sing about that second
realm given the human soul

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:30.070 line:84%
to purge its sin and grow
worthy to climb to paradise.

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:35.920 line:84%
Here rise to life
again dead poetry."

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.050 line:84%
It's a beautiful,
striking opening.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:40.180 line:84%
So probably the most
interesting line

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.600 line:84%
is that last one, though,
"Here rise to life

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.690 line:90%
again dead poetry."

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:48.760 line:84%
And I wish someone had
told me at least a little

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:52.690 line:84%
bit earlier that we were
reading dead poetry.

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:54.520 line:90%
But we learn about it now--

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.450 line:90%
or so it seems.

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:59.140 line:84%
It's not entirely clear
what that line means.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:03.760 line:84%
At a minimum, here on
Easter Sunday morning,

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.540 line:84%
poetry itself is going
to rise to new life.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:12.070 line:84%
No more poetry of death,
no more poetry of despair,

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:15.940 line:90%
no more poetry of the lost.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.670 line:84%
While the poem is
certainly about the state

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:23.320 line:84%
of souls after death and
these three parts of eternity,

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:25.940 line:84%
it's also about
the present moment.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:28.240 line:90%
It's about the here and now.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.550 line:84%
And so one of the great
fruits of the poem

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:34.930 line:84%
is to help us, the readers,
in the present moment

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:40.420 line:84%
of our lives, in our present
duties in the here and now.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:42.160 line:84%
One of the things
Dante does better

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:49.900 line:84%
than anyone is to help give the
reader a perspective on life

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:53.390 line:84%
that's reliable and
trustworthy and helpful.

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:55.390 line:84%
It's very easy to lose
perspective in life.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:58.600 line:84%
It's very easy to have
a skewed perspective.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:03.160 line:84%
And what Dante does is he
gives us an eternal perspective

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:06.610 line:84%
on the soul, on freedom,
on love, on God,

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:09.670 line:84%
and that's one of the greatest
gifts any teacher could

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:12.850 line:84%
give to a student,
any poet to a reader,

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:16.910 line:90%
any artist to an audience.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:20.500 line:84%
The beginning of
Dante's "Purgatorio."

